    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field derived from an owner's city using the fixed table (Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD, otherwise `UNKNOWN`), following the app's existing Spring/MapStruct/OpenAPI conventions.\n\nThree changes:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `locality` string property to the `Owner` schema (alongside the other derived fields like `membershipTier`), which regenerates `OwnerDto` with a `locality` getter/setter.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a `CITY_REGION` table constant and a `@Transient` derived getter `getLocality()` that returns the canonical region for the current city, or `\"UNKNOWN\"`. MapStruct auto-maps this into `OwnerDto.locality` by property name \u2014 no explicit mapping needed.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 No mapping annotation required; the property maps automatically.\n\n### One notable pitfall I hit and fixed\nMy first attempt put a `localityForCity(String): String` helper method on the `OwnerMapper` interface and referenced it via a MapStruct `expression`. MapStruct treated that `String\u2192String` method as a candidate *conversion* mapping and silently applied it to the `telephone` field during `OwnerFieldsDto \u2192 Owner` mapping, rewriting the phone number to `\"UNKNOWN\"`. That made `POST /api/owners` fail E.164 validation and return `400`. Deriving the value on the domain object instead of via a mapper helper avoids that entirely.",
